s_a_runtime_substrate_for_capability_controlled_self_evolving_llm_agents.pdf) | Screened |\n| DriftNet: A Dual-Head Trajectory Transformer for Detecting and Localizing Prompt Injection in LLM Agents | Asif Pinjari, Mithun Paul Saint-Germain | 2026 | [2609.10892_driftnet_a_dual_head_trajectory_transformer_for_detecting_and_localizing_prompt_injection_.pdf](papers/2609.10892_driftnet_a_dual_head_trajectory_transformer_for_detecting_and_localizing_prompt_injection_.pdf) | Screened |\n| The LLMbda Calculus: AI Agents, Conversations, and Information Flow | Zac Garby, Andrew D. Gordon, David Sands | 2026 | [2602.20064_the_llmbda_calculus_ai_agents_conversations_and_information_flow.pdf](papers/2602.20064_the_llmbda_calculus_ai_agents_conversations_and_information_flow.pdf) | Screened |\n| Amplify, Don't Create: Temporal Accumulation for Slow-Burn Prompt Injection | J. A. Corll | 2026 | [2606.20746_amplify_don_t_create_temporal_accumulation_for_slow_burn_prompt_injection.pdf](papers/2606.20746_amplify_don_t_create_temporal_accumulation_for_slow_burn_prompt_injection.pdf) | Screened |\n| Security Architecture for Agentic AI in Enterprise Cloud Environments: A Zero-Trust Framework for Secure Autonomous Systems | S. Suryawanshi | 2026 | [paper_security_architecture_for_agentic_ai_in_enterprise_cloud_environments_a_zero_trust_framewo.pdf](papers/paper_security_architecture_for_agentic_ai_in_enterprise_cloud_environments_a_zero_trust_framewo.pdf) | Screened |\n| Maris: A Formally Verifiable Privacy Policy Enforcement Paradigm for Multi-Agent Collaboration Systems | Jian Cui, Zi-Chuan Li, Chi Wang, Lu-Yi Xing, Xiao-Jing Liao | 2025 | [2505.04799_maris_a_formally_verifiable_privacy_policy_enforcement_paradigm_for_multi_agent_collaborat.pdf](papers/2505.04799_maris_a_formally_verifiable_privacy_policy_enforcement_paradigm_for_multi_agent_collaborat.pdf) | Screened |\n| Not what you've signed up for: Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection | Greshake, Kai; Abdelnabi, Sahar; Mishra, Shailesh; Endres, Christoph; Holz, Thorsten; Fritz, Mario | 2023 | [2302.12173_indirect_prompt_injection.pdf](papers/2302.12173_indirect_prompt_injection.pdf) | Screened |\n| Defeating Prompt Injections by Design | Debenedetti, Edoardo; Shumailov, Ilia; Fan, Tianqi; Hayes, Jamie; Carlini, Nicholas; Fabian, Daniel; Kern, Christoph; Shi, Chongyang; Terzis, Andreas; Tramèr, Florian | 2025 | [2503.18813_camel.pdf](papers/2503.18813_camel.pdf) | Screened |\n---\nname: safety-evals\ndescription: Run standardised AI-safety evaluations (Inspect / inspect_evals tasks, local vLLM serving, LLM judges, activation probes) and write results in the neurico-safety-result schema plus candidate findings. Use for any experiment that measures refusal, jailbreak robustness, honesty, sycophancy, bias, deception, dangerous-capability proxies, or builds a probe/monitor.\ntags:\n  - safety\n  - evaluation\n  - inspect\n  - vllm\n  - probes\n  - red-teaming\n---\n\n# safety-evals\n\nTested runners for safety evaluations. Prefer these scripts over hand-written loops: they\nproduce the result schema the safety engine ingests, keep judges and model versions on record,\nand resume when interrupted.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Shared evaluation interpreter: `$NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python` (inspect_ai, inspect_evals,\n  vllm, torch, transformers, scikit-learn). When the variable is unset (local run outside the\n  engine) create a venv and `uv pip install inspect-ai inspect-evals vllm transformers scikit-learn`.\n- Knowledge base (read-only): `$NEURICO_SAFETY_KB` (`catalog/evals.yaml`, `catalog/models.yaml`,\n  per-eval notes under `evals/`, gap analyses under `gaps/`, lab summaries under `labs/`).\n- GPU: use the GPU(s) in `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`; do not pick others.\n- Provider keys come from the environment (`OPENROUTER_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `HF_TOKEN`);\n  never print or write them.\n\n## Scripts\n\n| Script | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `scripts/run_eval.py --evals <ids> --models <ids> [--limit N]` | Run catalog evals against catalog models (serves vLLM models itself, starts the local judge if needed); writes `results/safety/<eval>__<model>.json` |\n| `scripts/serve_model.py <hf_id or catalog id> [--port P]` | Start an OpenAI-compatible vLLM endpoint for ad-hoc experiments; writes `.neurico/model_endpoint.json`; stop with Ctrl-C or `--stop` |\n| `scripts/probe.py --model <hf_id> --data <jsonl> [--layer L] [--pool last|mean]` | Extract activations, train a logistic-regression probe with a held-out split, report AUROC / TPR@FPR with bootstrap CIs, save the probe |\n| `scripts/judge_calibration.py --judge <catalog id> --labelled <jsonl>` | Agreement of a judge against human labels (accuracy, kappa, TPR@FPR) |\n| `scripts/check_finding.py findings/<slug>.yaml` | Validate a candidate finding file before you finish |\n\nRun every script with the evaluation interpreter, e.g.\n`$NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python .claude/skills/safety-evals/scripts/run_eval.py --evals xstest-safe --models qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct --limit 100`.\n\n## Custom evaluations\n\nWhen the catalog has no entry for what you need, write an Inspect task (`inspect_ai.Task`\nwith a dataset, solver and scorer) under `src/evals/`, run it with\n`$NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python -m inspect_ai eval src/evals/<task>.py --model <model> --log-dir results/safety/inspect_logs --log-format json`,\nand convert the log with `scripts/run_eval.py --from-log <log.json> --eval-id <id> --model-id <id>`\nso the result lands in the standard schema. Add the new eval to your REPORT.md with its source.\n\n## Results schema (`neurico-safety-result/v1`)\n\nSee `references/result-schema.md`. Essentials: `eval_id`, `model_id`, `n`, `metrics` (every\nmetric with its stderr where available), `judge`, `task_args`, `command`, `versions`,\n`inspect_log`, `status`, `error`. One file per eval x model.\n\n## Findings\n\nA finding is a behaviour worth reporting (new, unexpected, or a quantified gap). Write\n`findings/<slug>.yaml` (schema in `references/finding-schema.md`) with evidence that cites\nresult files and transcript paths, and a reproduction command. The engine files it as a\n`candidate`; an independent validation job decides whether it becomes `confirmed`. Do not\nclaim confirmation yourself.\n\n## Rules that keep results trustworthy\n\n1. Report n and SE for every number; compare models on identical items.\n2. Judges are calibrated (`judge_calibration.py`) and their agreement goes in the report.\n3. Pair refusal with over-refusal and capability with a baseline.\n4. Label runs on 0.5B-3B models as pipeline tests, not cross-family claims.\n5. No real hazardous content: use proxies; store anything harmful under `results/restricted/` (git-ignored).\n6. Never kill processes by name pattern on this shared machine; stop only the servers you started\n   (`serve_model.py --stop` or the PID in `.neurico/model_endpoint.json`).\n---\nname: azure-models\ndescription: Call GPT models on the lab's Azure OpenAI resource (and Claude on Azure AI Foundry when deployed) with the keys already in the environment. Present only when Azure is active for this run; use it for any experiment that queries OpenAI-family models, judges, or graders instead of OpenRouter.\ntags:\n  - azure\n  - openai\n  - anthropic\n  - api\n  - inference\n---\n\n# azure-models\n\nAzure is active for this run (`NEURICO_AZURE=1`). Prefer Azure for OpenAI-family models;\nkeep OpenRouter for models that are not deployed on Azure (open-weight models, Gemini,\nand so on). Never print or write the key values.\n\n## Endpoints\n\n| Family | Client | Base URL | Key env var | Model argument |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OpenAI (GPT) | `openai.OpenAI` | `$AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL` (`https://<resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1`) | `AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY` | the **deployment name** |\n| Claude on Foundry | `anthropic.AnthropicFoundry` | `$ANTHROPIC_FOUNDRY_BASE_URL` | `ANTHROPIC_FOUNDRY_API_KEY` (falls back to the Azure key) | the deployment name |\n\nDeployments that exist today (checked 2026-10-20): `gpt-6-astra`, `gpt-5.6-sol`, `gpt-5.4`,\n`gpt-4.1-mini`. `AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENTS` (if set) lists the ones you may use. No Claude\ndeployment answered on the Foundry resource on 2026-10-20; run the probe before relying on one.\n\n## Scripts\n\n| Script | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `scripts/list_azure_models.py [--probe name ...]` | Which deployments answer right now (sends a 1-token request to each candidate) |\n| `scripts/azure_client.py` | `openai_client()` and `anthropic_client()` helpers plus `chat(model, prompt)`; import it or run it for a smoke test |\n\n## Usage\n\n```python\nimport os, openai\nclient = openai.OpenAI(base_url=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL\"], api_key=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"])\nresp = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"gpt-4.1-mini\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}])\n```\n\nInspect (`inspect_ai`): `--model openai-api/azure/gpt-4.1-mini` with\n`AZURE_BASE_URL=$AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL` and `AZURE_API_KEY=$AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY` exported\n(the safety-evals suite runner does this for `azure:<deployment>` entries in the model catalog).\n\nRecord the deployment name and the `model` string the API returns (it carries the dated\nversion, for example `gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14`) in your results and REPORT.md.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Azure first for GPT-family calls; OpenRouter only for models Azure does not serve.\n- Keys stay in environment variables; never copy them into code, notebooks, logs or results.\n- Cost: Azure is pay-as-you-go on the lab's subscription. Estimate token volume before large\n  sweeps and use `gpt-4.1-mini` for development and judging unless the research needs a\n  frontier model.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
