{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd\ncat \"$NEURICO_SAFETY_KB/evals/agentdojo.md\" \"$NEURICO_SAFETY_KB/evals/agentharm.md\"\ncat \"$NEURICO_SAFETY_KB/catalog/models.yaml\" | head -180\nls /net/spaces/chenhao/chai-lab-models/haokunliu/safety/hf_cache\nfind .neurico scripts artifacts -maxdepth 2 -type f | head -60\nsed -n '\"'1,180p' .codex/skills/safety-evals/scripts/serve_model.py\nsed -n '1,110p' resources.md\"","aggregated_output":"/net/spaces/chenhao/chai-lab-models/haokunliu/safety/workspaces/benign-tool-injection-failures-3246-codex-gpt-6-astra\n# AgentDojo (ETH SPY Lab) — prompt-injection robustness for tool agents\n\nFetched: 2026-10-19. Sources: https://github.com/ethz-spylab/agentdojo (MIT, pushed 2026-06-02),\nhttps://agentdojo.spylab.ai/ , paper https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352 , inspect_evals/agentdojo\nREADME. Extended by US AISI in a joint red-teaming exercise with UK AISI (Inspect README).\n\nWhat it measures: \"97 realistic tasks\" and \"629 security test cases\"; current LLMs \"fail at many\ntasks (even in the absence of attacks)\"; utility and targeted attack success. Also a ControlArena\nsetting.\n\n## Tooling\n- Upstream: `pip install agentdojo` (+ `pip install \"agentdojo[transformers]\"` for the PI\n  detector); `python -m agentdojo.scripts.benchmark -s workspace -ut user_task_0 -ut user_task_1\n  --model gpt-4o-2024-05-13 --defense tool_filter --attack tool_knowledge`; all suites:\n  `python -m agentdojo.scripts.benchmark --model gpt-4o-2024-05-13 --defense tool_filter --attack\n  tool_knowledge`. \"the API of the package is still under development and might change\".\n  Supported providers are not listed on the README/site (UNVERIFIED beyond OpenAI example).\n- Inspect port: `inspect eval inspect_evals/agentdojo --model <m>`; `-T workspace=banking`;\n  `-T with_injections=false` (benign utility only); `-T attack=important_instructions` (default);\n  `-T with_sandbox_tasks=only`; `-T user_task_ids=[1,2,3] -T injection_task_ids=[1,2]`;\n  `-T agent=default -T agent_kwargs='{\"temperature\": 0.7}'`. 5 task suites.\n- Compute: 97 user tasks × injection variants; agentic, several tool calls each.\n- Small-model OK: tool calling required; benign-utility run with `-T with_injections=false` is\n  the right first step for a 1.5B-3B model.\n- Used by: ETH Zurich SPY Lab (https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352 ; see third_party/academic-and-nonprofit-auditors.md); Gray Swan AI maintains a fork (https://github.com/GraySwanAI)\n\n## Used by\n- Used by: xAI (ASR: Grok 4 0.02, Code Fast 1 26.9%, Grok 4 Fast 0.00/0.03, 4.1 0.05/0.01, 4.20 0.33; not reported in 4.5-4.7; https://data.x.ai/2025-08-20-grok-4-model-card.pdf ; https://data.x.ai/2026-04-07-grok-4-20-model-card.pdf)\n- Used by: Meta (LlamaFirewall paper, Prompt Guard 2 card, Muse Spark reports; https://ai.meta.com/static-resource/muse-spark-safety-and-preparedness-report/)\n- Used by: US CAISI agent hijacking evaluations built on AgentDojo (https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2025/01/technical-blog-strengthening-ai-agent-hijacking-evaluations)\n- Used by: UK AISI Inspect Evals (https://github.com/UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_evals)\n# AgentHarm (UK AISI)\n\nFetched: 2026-10-19\n\n- What it measures: Harmful agentic task compliance: 110 base (440 augmented) multi-step tool-use tasks across 11 harm categories, plus benign twins to measure over-refusal of agents.\n- Metric: Harm score (% of harmful tasks completed, with/without jailbreak template) and refusal rate; benign-task completion for capability.\n- Open-source: yes (in inspect_evals)\n- Link: https://github.com/UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_evals/tree/main/src/inspect_evals/agentharm ; paper https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09024\n- Runnable on a 0.5B-7B open model: yes (needs tool-calling; 1.5B-7B instruct models can run it with synthetic tools)\n- Notes: Not used by any of the four Chinese labs in their own reports (as of 2026-10-19); listed because it is the obvious third-party agentic-misuse test to run on them.\n\nUsed by: (none of Qwen / Kimi / GLM / DeepSeek self-report it; proposed in gaps/open-model-families-gap-matrix.md)\n- Used by: Gray Swan AI (/data/haokun_test/neurico/safety_kb/third_party/gray-swan.md)\n\n## Tooling (appended 2026-10-19)\n\nFetched: 2026-10-19. Sources: inspect_evals/agentharm README\n(https://github.com/UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_evals/blob/main/src/inspect_evals/agentharm/README.md),\ndataset https://huggingface.co/datasets/ai-safety-institute/AgentHarm (licence \"other\", not\ngated per HF API). https://github.com/ai-safety-institute/AgentHarm returned 404.\n\nWhat it measures: harmful agentic tasks with synthetic tools, plus a benign twin. Public release:\n\"44 out of 66 public test base behaviors (176 augmented ones)\" and \"8 out of 11 validation base\nbehaviors (32 augmented ones)\". Metrics: `avg_score`, `avg_refusals`, `avg_score_non_refusals`.\nLicence: \"MIT License with an additional clause\" limiting use to improving AI safety/security.\n\n### Tooling details\n- `uv run inspect eval inspect_evals/agentharm --model openai/gpt-5-nano` ;\n  `inspect_evals/agentharm_benign` ; `inspect eval-set inspect_evals/agentharm inspect_evals/agentharm_benign`.\n- `-T` options: `agent` (default `'default'`; `-T agent=refusal`), `behavior_ids`, `split`\n  (`'test_public'` default, `'val'`, `'test_private'`), `detailed_behaviors`, `hint_included`,\n  `n_irrelevant_tools` (default 0), `refusal_judge`, `semantic_judge` (both default\n  `'openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06'`), `chat_dataset` (agentharm only), `agent_kwargs`.\n- Interfaces: any Inspect provider that supports tool calling. For Qwen small models via vLLM\n  this requires the vLLM tool-call parser (flags not verified here — UNVERIFIED).\n- Compute: 176 + 176 rollouts, ≤ a few tool calls each, plus 2 judge calls per sample.\n- Small-model OK: partially; expect many malformed tool calls at 0.5B — treat it as a pipeline\n  smoke test, not a result.\n\n## Used by\n- Used by: xAI (no-attack answer rate: Grok 4 0.14, Code Fast 1 17.0%, 4 Fast 0.08, 4.1 0.14, 4.20 0.30; https://data.x.ai/2025-08-20-grok-4-model-card.pdf ; https://data.x.ai/2026-04-07-grok-4-20-model-card.pdf)\n- Used by: Meta (Muse Spark reports: AgentHarm ASR 26.0 -> 3.4 in 1.1, benign-subset FRR; https://ai.meta.com/static-resource/muse-spark-safety-and-preparedness-report/)\n- Used by: Mistral (Mistral Large 4 launch, cyber-prompt refusal rate; https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4)\n- Used by: UK AISI (authored; https://www.aisi.gov.uk/research/agentharm-a-benchmark-for-measuring-harmfulness-of-llm-agents ; inspect_evals agentharm)\n# Model registry for the safety engine.\n#\n# access forms:\n#   vllm:<hf_id>         served locally by the safety-evals skill (needs gpu_memory_gb)\n#   openrouter:<slug>    OpenRouter (OPENROUTER_KEY)\n#   anthropic:<id>       Anthropic API (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n#   openai:<id>          OpenAI API (OPENAI_API_KEY)\n# test_tier: true marks small members used to exercise the pipeline on this server.\nmodels:\n  - id: qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\n    params_b: 0.5\n    gpu_memory_gb: 6\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Apache-2.0\n  - id: qwen2.5-1.5b-instruct\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\n    params_b: 1.5\n    gpu_memory_gb: 8\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Apache-2.0\n  - id: qwen2.5-3b-instruct\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct\n    params_b: 3\n    gpu_memory_gb: 12\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Qwen Research\n  - id: qwen3-0.6b\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B\n    params_b: 0.6\n    gpu_memory_gb: 6\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Apache-2.0\n    notes: thinking model; suites disable thinking via chat template kwargs\n  - id: qwen3-1.7b\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B\n    params_b: 1.7\n    gpu_memory_gb: 8\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Apache-2.0\n  - id: qwen3-4b\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-4B\n    params_b: 4\n    gpu_memory_gb: 14\n    test_tier: true\n    license: Apache-2.0\n  # API models (judges and subjects). Fill in exact slugs from catalog research.\n  - id: claude-opus-5-5\n    family: claude\n    vendor: Anthropic\n    access: anthropic:claude-opus-5-5\n    api: true\n  - id: claude-fable-5-1\n    family: claude\n    vendor: Anthropic\n    access: anthropic:claude-fable-5-1\n    api: true\n  - id: gpt-4.1-mini-judge\n    family: gpt\n    vendor: OpenAI\n    access: openrouter:openai/gpt-4.1-mini\n    api: true\n    role: judge-api\n    notes: cheap API judge; select with NEURICO_SAFETY_JUDGE=gpt-4.1-mini-judge\n  - id: qwen2.5-7b-judge\n    family: qwen\n    vendor: Alibaba\n    access: vllm:Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\n    params_b: 7\n    gpu_memory_gb: 20\n    gpu_memory_utilization: 0.42\n    max_model_len: 8192\n    role: judge\n    license: Apache-2.0\n    notes: default local judge (no API spend); calibration numbers live in evals/judge-calibration.md\n  # Azure OpenAI deployments on the lab resource (checked 2026-10-20); need NEURICO_AZURE=1 keys\n  - id: azure-gpt-6-astra\n    family: gpt\n    vendor: OpenAI (Azure)\n    access: azure:gpt-6-astra\n    api: true\n  - id: azure-gpt-5.6-sol\n    family: gpt\n    vendor: OpenAI (Azure)\n    access: azure:gpt-5.6-sol\n    api: true\n  - id: azure-gpt-5.4\n    family: gpt\n    vendor: OpenAI (Azure)\n    access: azure:gpt-5.4\n    api: true\n  - id: azure-gpt-4.1-mini-judge\n    family: gpt\n    vendor: OpenAI (Azure)\n    access: azure:gpt-4.1-mini\n    api: true\n    role: judge-api\n    notes: API judge on Azure; select with NEURICO_SAFETY_JUDGE=azure-gpt-4.1-mini-judge\n  # Larger open-weight members for the DSI cluster (A100 80GB / H100 / H200), added 2026-10-20\n  - {id: qwen3-8b, family: qwen, vendor: Alibaba, access: \"vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-8B\", params_b: 8, gpu_memory_gb: 24, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.6, max_model_len: 8192, license: Apache-2.0}\n  - {id: qwen3-14b, family: qwen, vendor: Alibaba, access: \"vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-14B\", params_b: 14, gpu_memory_gb: 40, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.7, max_model_len: 8192, license: Apache-2.0}\n  - {id: qwen3-32b, family: qwen, vendor: Alibaba, access: \"vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-32B\", params_b: 32, gpu_memory_gb: 75, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.9, max_model_len: 8192, license: Apache-2.0}\n  - {id: qwen3-30b-a3b, family: qwen, vendor: Alibaba, access: \"vllm:Qwen/Qwen3-30B-A3B\", params_b: 30, gpu_memory_gb: 70, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.9, max_model_len: 8192, license: Apache-2.0, notes: \"MoE, 3B active\"}\n  - {id: moonlight-16b-a3b-instruct, family: kimi, vendor: Moonshot AI, access: \"vllm:moonshotai/Moonlight-16B-A3B-Instruct\", params_b: 16, gpu_memory_gb: 40, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.7, max_model_len: 8192, serve_args: [\"--trust-remote-code\"], license: MIT, notes: \"smallest Kimi-family text model with open weights\"}\n  - {id: deepseek-r1-distill-qwen-7b, family: deepseek, vendor: DeepSeek, access: \"vllm:deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B\", params_b: 7, gpu_memory_gb: 24, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.6, max_model_len: 8192, license: MIT}\n  - {id: deepseek-r1-distill-qwen-14b, family: deepseek, vendor: DeepSeek, access: \"vllm:deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-14B\", params_b: 14, gpu_memory_gb: 40, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.7, max_model_len: 8192, license: MIT}\n  - {id: deepseek-r1-distill-llama-8b, family: deepseek, vendor: DeepSeek, access: \"vllm:deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B\", params_b: 8, gpu_memory_gb: 24, gpu_memory_utilization: 0.6, max_model_len: 8192, license: MIT}\ndatasets\nhub\nxet\n.neurico/idea.yaml\n.neurico/research_environment.json\n.neurico/pipeline_state.json\nscripts/prepare_datasets.py\nscripts/catalog_resources.py\nscripts/__pycache__/prepare_datasets.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/__pycache__/build_pilot.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/__pycache__/validate_resources.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/__pycache__/prepare_models.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/__pycache__/download_papers.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/__pycache__/catalog_resources.cpython-312.pyc\nscripts/validate_resources.py\nscripts/prepare_models.py\nscripts/download_papers.py\nscripts/build_pilot.py\nartifacts/screening.json\nartifacts/moonlight.json\nartifacts/literature_chunk_notes.md\nartifacts/2302.12173_indirect_prompt_injection_chunk_001.png\nartifacts/2302.12173_indirect_prompt_injection.txt\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_001.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent.txt\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_006.png\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_002.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_008.png\nartifacts/2503.18813_camel.txt\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_005.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_010.png\nartifacts/2312.14197_bipia.txt\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_004.png\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_003.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_009.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_007.png\nartifacts/pilot_manifest.json\nartifacts/deepseek.json\nartifacts/2503.18813_camel_chunk_001.png\nartifacts/qwen3.json\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_011.png\nartifacts/paper_downloads.json\nartifacts/dataset_manifest.json\nartifacts/search_log.md\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_012.png\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_007.png\nartifacts/model_manifest.json\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_009.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_003.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_004.png\nartifacts/hf_injecagent.json\nartifacts/resource_validation.json\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo.txt\nartifacts/paper_resolution.json\nartifacts/hf_agentdojo.json\nartifacts/2312.14197_bipia_chunk_001.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_005.png\nartifacts/semantic_search.json\nartifacts/paper_metadata.json\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_008.png\nartifacts/2403.02691_injecagent_chunk_002.png\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_006.png\nartifacts/2406.13352_agentdojo_chunk_001.png\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Serve a model with vLLM as an OpenAI-compatible endpoint for ad-hoc experiments.\n\n    $NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python serve_model.py Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct [--name qwen05] [--port 8450]\n    $NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python serve_model.py qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct     # catalog id works too\n    $NEURICO_SAFETY_VENV/bin/python serve_model.py --stop                    # stop the server you started\n\nWrites `.neurico/model_endpoint.json` ({base_url, model, pid}) in the workspace. The server\nruns in the foreground; run it in the background with `nohup ... &` or a separate shell and\nstop it with `--stop` (never kill by process name on a shared machine).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport os\nimport signal\nimport sys\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\nfrom _common import add_safety_package, workspace_root  # noqa: E402\n\nadd_safety_package()\nfrom safety.kb import KB  # noqa: E402\nfrom safety.serving import VLLMServer  # noqa: E402\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)\n    parser.add_argument(\"model\", nargs=\"?\", help=\"HF id or catalog model id\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--name\", help=\"served model name (default: catalog id or HF basename)\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--port\", type=int)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--max-model-len\", type=int, default=4096)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--gpu-memory-utilization\", type=float, default=0.45)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--extra\", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER, default=[], help=\"extra vLLM args after --extra\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--stop\", action=\"store_true\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    root = workspace_root()\n    endpoint_file = root / \".neurico\" / \"model_endpoint.json\"\n    if args.stop:\n        if not endpoint_file.exists():\n            print(\"no endpoint file; nothing to stop\")\n            return 0\n        info = json.loads(endpoint_file.read_text())\n        try:\n            os.killpg(int(info[\"pid\"]), signal.SIGTERM)\n            print(f\"stopped server pid {info['pid']}\")\n        except ProcessLookupError:\n            print(\"server already gone\")\n        endpoint_file.unlink()\n        return 0\n    if not args.model:\n        parser.error(\"model is required\")\n\n    hf_id, name = args.model, args.name\n    try:\n        entry = KB().models().get(args.model)\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - no knowledge base available\n        entry = None\n    if entry and str(entry.get(\"access\", \"\")).startswith(\"vllm:\"):\n        hf_id = entry[\"access\"].split(\":\", 1)[1]\n        name = name or entry[\"id\"]\n    name = name or hf_id.split(\"/\")[-1].lower()\n    gpu = (os.environ.get(\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\") or \"\").split(\",\")[0] or None\n    server = VLLMServer(hf_id, name, gpu=gpu, python=sys.executable, log_file=root / \"logs\" / f\"vllm_{name}.log\",\n                        gpu_memory_utilization=args.gpu_memory_utilization, max_model_len=args.max_model_len,\n                        extra_args=args.extra, port=args.port, hf_home=os.environ.get(\"HF_HOME\"))\n    server.start()\n    endpoint_file.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    endpoint_file.write_text(json.dumps({\"base_url\": server.base_url, \"model\": name, \"hf_id\": hf_id,\n                                         \"pid\": server.process.pid, \"inspect_model\": f\"openai/{name}\"}, indent=2))\n    print(f\"ready: {server.base_url} model={name} (endpoint written to {endpoint_file})\", flush=True)\n    print(\"set OPENAI_BASE_URL to the base_url and OPENAI_API_KEY=[REDACTED] for inspect/openai clients\", flush=True)\n    try:\n        while server.process.poll() is None:\n            time.sleep(5)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        server.stop()\n        if endpoint_file.exists():\n            endpoint_file.unlink()\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n# Resources catalog\n\nValidated local resources for **Benign tool-injection failures with matched utility controls**. Prepared on October 20, 2026. Start with `planning.md`, `literature_review.md`, and the phase notes in `STATE.md`. No model experiment has been run.\n\n## Resource summary\n\n| Resource | Count / size | Location | Validation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Full paper PDFs | 99 files; 97 service records plus 2 supplements | `papers/` | All parse; hashes/page counts saved |\n| Downloaded benchmark resources | 2 datasets, 21 data files, 4,999,157 bytes | `datasets/InjecAgent/`, `datasets/agentdojo/` | Schemas, counts, YAML includes and hashes checked |\n| Code repositories | 2 clean pinned clones | `code/agentdojo/`, `code/InjecAgent/` | README/dependencies/entry points inspected |\n| Generated pilot | 24 development worlds, 6 templates | `datasets/benign_record_pilot.jsonl` | 72 checkpoint-specific token-matched I/Q pairs |\n| Tokenizer/config/card resources | 3 models, 13 files; no weights | `datasets/tokenizers/` | Immutable revisions and hashes |\n| Isolated environment | Fresh project `.venv` | `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` | Preparation scripts executed successfully |\n\n## Datasets and code\n\n| Name | Source | Purpose | Local path | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| InjecAgent data | https://github.com/uiuc-kang-lab/InjecAgent | 17 user templates; 1,054 cases per base/enhanced setting | `datasets/InjecAgent/` | MIT; adaptation required for benign records; no separate clear-data release found |\n| AgentDojo data | https://github.com/ethz-spylab/agentdojo | Four synthetic environment suites and injection vectors | `datasets/agentdojo/` | MIT; executable task predicates remain in code |\n| AgentDojo code | https://github.com/ethz-spylab/agentdojo | Stateful tools, user/attacker checks, counter benchmark example | `code/agentdojo/` | Commit `089ed468cf3ed0322acc66b0211f26d9d90dbf60`; sparse checkout |\n| InjecAgent code | https://github.com/uiuc-kang-lab/InjecAgent | ReAct and function-call evaluation and parsing | `code/InjecAgent/` | Commit `f19c9f2c79a41046eb13c03c51a24c567a8ffa07`; NLTK import absent from requirements |\n\nDownload commands, loading instructions, schemas, licenses and caveats are in `datasets/README.md` and `code/README.md`. Run `bash datasets/download.sh` at the repository root after restoring pinned clones and `uv sync --project .`. Data are local and git-ignored; `datasets/examples.json` is a small trackable sample. Original repository-embedded assets remain in the clones; canonical experiment data and literature PDFs are in their dedicated directories.\n\n## Checkpoints\n\n| Role | Official model | Observed revision | Local assets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary | Qwen/Qwen3-8B | `b968826d9c46dd6066d109eabc6255188de91218` | `datasets/tokenizers/qwen3/` |\n| Secondary (proposed) | deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B | `916b56a44061fd5cd7d6a8fb632557ed4f724f60` | `datasets/tokenizers/deepseek/` |\n| Secondary | moonshotai/Moonlight-16B-A3B-Instruct | `4e735b07a89f73647dfab71ab91b840f362ede5b` | `datasets/tokenizers/moonlight/` |\n\nDeepSeek family was underspecified; the 7B Qwen-derived distill checkpoint is a documented proposed choice, not an author-specified exact model. Do not interpret it as an independent architecture family. Moonlight uses a custom tiktoken tokenizer; historical model-card dependency recommendations are not a tested common serving stack. The runner must validate native tool interfaces and full chat serialization. GPU allocation, inference compatibility, model weights and clean task capability were not tested.\n\n## Evidence paths and search limitations\n\n- `artifacts/search_log.md`: search order, commands, sources and retrieval failures.\n- `paper_search_results/`: complete original service results, including abstracts.\n- `artifacts/paper_resolution.json`, `paper_downloads.json`, `screening.json`: identifiers, download outcomes and all screening decisions.\n- `artifacts/literature_chunk_notes.md`: immediate sequential notes from all 21 core-paper chunks.\n- `artifacts/dataset_manifest.json`, `model_manifest.json`: URLs, revisions, bytes and hashes.\n- `artifacts/resource_validation.json`: all PDF/data checks and clean code revisions.\n- `artifacts/pilot_manifest.json`: fixture provenance and token-matching scope.\n\nFive candidate full texts remained unavailable after retries; their available abstracts were screened and none blocks the core design. Titles and statuses follow. Do not imply full review of them.\n\n- TrustTrace: Provenance-Aware Tracing of Indirect Prompt Injection Propagation in LLM-Assisted Learning Workflows: failed; No PDF from alternate/retry source.\n- [Short Paper] Forensic Analysis of Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on LLM Agents: no_open_pdf; No PDF from alternate/retry source.\n- Execution-Aware Stage-Wise Evaluation Framework for Indirect Prompt Injection in LLM-Based Email Agents: failed; No PDF from alternate/retry source.\n- AgentBreaker: Evaluating Context-Aware Indirect Prompt Injection Risks in Modern Web Agents: failed; No PDF from alternate/retry source.\n- Temporal Dynamics of Memory Poisoning in Web3-Style LLM Agents: failed; No PDF from alternate/retry source.\n\n## Experiment recommendation\n\n1. Primary: frozen paired injection/quotation comparison on Qwen3-8B, with clean eligibility determined separately and explicit user-authorized target edits as capability controls. Use deterministic fictional state, not real services.\n2. Secondary: same locked logical cases on the proposed DeepSeek and specified Moonlight checkpoints; report checkpoint-specific and shared-intersection clean eligibility.\n3. Secondary: a single boundary-reminder ablation, reporting both unauthorized mutations and legitimate utility.\n\nThese are the only three implementation directions retained. The full scoring table and rejected directions are in `planning.md`. Treat any executed unauthorized mutation as the primary event; retain exact-target success and attempts separately. Match serialized tool-result token lengths per model; the prepared pilot already matches those lengths but still needs full-chat verification. Use paired confidence intervals, template clustering, and the 0.10 threshold rather than only a zero-difference test. Six development templates cannot support confirmatory generalization. No effect size, power, clean eligibility, or checkpoint ranking has been measured.\n\n## Full paper inventory\n\nThe table catalogs all 99 full PDF files. Titles/author spellings for service records come from the service metadata; verify the publication PDF before final bibliography submission. Related versions must be deduplicated as studies. Detailed screening/methodology relevance notes and code links are in `papers/README.md`.\n\n| Title | Authors | Year | File | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| InjecAgent: Benchmarking Indirect Prompt Injections in Tool-Integrated Large Language Model Agents | Qiusi Zhan, Zhi-Xiang Liang, Zifan Ying, Daniel Kang | 2024 | [2403.02691_injecagent.pdf](papers/2403.02691_injecagent.pdf) | Full PDF |\n| Adaptive Attacks Break Defenses Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on LLM Agents | Qiusi Zhan, Richard Fang, H. Panchal, Daniel Kang | 2025 | [2503.00061_adaptive_attacks_break_defenses_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_on_llm_agents.pdf](papers/2503.00061_adaptive_attacks_break_defenses_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_on_llm_agents.pdf) | Screened |\n| AgentDojo: A Dynamic Environment to Evaluate Attacks and Defenses for LLM Agents | Edoardo Debenedetti, Jie Zhang, Mislav Balunovic, Luca Beurer-Kellner, Marc Fischer, Florian Tramèr | 2024 | [2406.13352_agentdojo.pdf](papers/2406.13352_agentdojo.pdf) | Full PDF |\n| The Task Shield: Enforcing Task Alignment to Defend Against Indirect Prompt Injection in LLM Agents | Feiran Jia, Tong Wu, Xin Qin, A. Squicciarini | 2024 | [2412.16682_the_task_shield_enforcing_task_alignment_to_defend_against_indirect_prompt_injection_in_ll.pdf](papers/2412.16682_the_task_shield_enforcing_task_alignment_to_defend_against_indirect_prompt_injection_in_ll.pdf) | Screened |\n| MELON: Provable Defense Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks in AI Agents | Kai-Jie Zhu, Xianjun Yang, Jin-Dong Wang, Wen-Bo Guo, William Yang Wang | 2025 | [2502.05174_melon_provable_defense_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_in_ai_agents.pdf](papers/2502.05174_melon_provable_defense_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_in_ai_agents.pdf) | Screened |\n| Progent: Securing AI Agents with Privilege Control | Tian-Neng Shi, Jingxuan He, Zhun Wang, Hong-Wei Li, Linyu Wu, Wen-Bo Guo, D. Song | 2025 | [2504.11703_progent_securing_ai_agents_with_privilege_control.pdf](papers/2504.11703_progent_securing_ai_agents_with_privilege_control.pdf) | Screened |\n| PromptArmor: Simple yet Effective Prompt Injection Defenses | Tian-Neng Shi, Kai-Jie Zhu, Zhun Wang, Yu-Qi Jia, Will Cai, Wei-Da Liang, Haonan Wang, Hend Alzahrani, Joshua Lu, Kenji Kawaguchi, Basel Alomair, Xuandong Zhao, William Yang Wang, N. Gong, Wen-Bo Guo, D. Song | 2025 | [2507.15219_promptarmor_simple_yet_effective_prompt_injection_defenses.pdf](papers/2507.15219_promptarmor_simple_yet_effective_prompt_injection_defenses.pdf) | Screened |\n| AgentDyn: Are Your Agent Security Defenses Deployable in Real-World Dynamic Environments? | Hao Li, Ruoyao Wen, Shang-Hao Shi, Ning Zhang, Yevgeniy Vorobeychik, Chao-Wei Xiao | 2026 | [2602.03117_agentdyn_are_your_agent_security_defenses_deployable_in_real_world_dynamic_environments.pdf](papers/2602.03117_agentdyn_are_your_agent_security_defenses_deployable_in_real_world_dynamic_environmen